{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '140,275p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v140; sed -n '285,390p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v285; sed -n '437,530p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v437; sed -n '744,805p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v744\"","aggregated_output":"   140\treads with `dao.jing/get` and a sentinel, performs no stream operation, and\n   141\tnever throws for absent or invalid content.\n   142\t\n   143\t```clojure\n   144\t(walk handle manifest-address)\n   145\t(walk handle manifest-address {:bounds {:max-parts n :max-depth d :max-bytes b}})\n   146\t\n   147\t;; exactly one of:\n   148\t{:yin.link.closure/outcome :missing\n   149\t :address a :role role :path [...]}\n   150\t{:yin.link.closure/outcome :complete\n   151\t :manifest manifest-address :blobs n :modules [manifest-address ...]\n   152\t :requires [{:module manifest-address :name 'other.lib :manifest pinned} ...]}\n   153\t{:yin.link.closure/outcome :invalid\n   154\t :address a :role role :path [...]\n   155\t :defect {:code code :detail {...}}}\n   156\t```\n   157\t\n   158\t`:requires` lists every `:yin.module/requires` entry of every module in the\n   159\tclosure, in walk order: the requiring module, the dependency's name, and the\n   160\taddress the publisher pinned.\n   161\t\n   162\t`:code` is one of a closed set. `:detail` is the underlying validator's\n   163\tdefect map when there is one; no consumer compares it.\n   164\t\n   165\t| `:code` | Raised by |\n   166\t|---|---|\n   167\t| `:address-mismatch` | A blob that fails `segment-matches?`. |\n   168\t| `:manifest-defect` | `manifest-defect`. |\n   169\t| `:row-defect` | `row-local-defect`. |\n   170\t| `:record-defect` | `record-defect`. |\n   171\t| `:derivation-mismatch` | A record whose `:yin.ledger/input` is not the tree. |\n   172\t| `:index-entry-missing` | An H or R with no `:yin.module/index` entry. |\n   173\t| `:identity-mismatch` | An image that fails its format's `:identity-matches-fn`. |\n   174\t| `:parts-limit` | A walk bound exceeded. |\n   175\t\n   176\t`role` is one of `:manifest`, `:row`, `:record`, `:image`, `:require`.\n   177\t`path` is the chain of manifest addresses from the root to the module the\n   178\tblob belongs to.\n   179\t\n   180\tThe closure of one manifest is, in walk order:\n   181\t\n   182\t| Step | Blob | Check before continuing |\n   183\t|---|---|---|\n   184\t| 1 | The manifest | `segment-matches?`; `manifest-defect` is nil. |\n   185\t| 2 | Every row of `:yin.module/tree` | A bounded worklist over the grammar's child slots (`ast-format`'s `:parts-fn`); each row `segment-matches?` and has no `row-local-defect`. |\n   186\t| 3 | The derivation record of each of `:yin.semantic/code`, `:yin.debruijn.code`, `:yin.debruijn.register` the manifest names | `segment-matches?`; `record-defect` is nil; `:yin.ledger/input` equals the tree. |\n   187\t| 4 | The image each record's `:yin.ledger/output` identifies | Its storage address is the identity itself for the semantic vector, and the `:yin.module/index` entry for H and R; an identity with no index entry is `:invalid`. The image satisfies its format record's `:identity-matches-fn`. |\n   188\t| 5 | The closure of every manifest under `:yin.module/requires` | Recursively, depth bounded; a manifest already visited is not walked again. |\n   189\t\n   190\t- **All four formats are walked, whichever one a request names.** A closure\n   191\t  is complete only when the tree and all three lowered images the manifest\n   192\t  names are local. A manifest that names fewer derivations has a smaller\n   193\t  closure; that is the publisher's choice and section 5.2 forbids it to this\n   194\t  epic's publisher.\n   195\t- `:yin.module/primitives` values are profile addresses compared at\n   196\t  discharge. They are not fetched and are not part of the closure.\n   197\t- The first absent blob ends the walk as `:missing`. The first failed check\n   198\t  ends it as `:invalid`. `:invalid` is terminal; fetching again cannot\n   199\t  repair content that hashes to its address and is wrong.\n   200\t- The walker verifies shape and address. It does not re-lower, discharge or\n   201\t  judge names: `link-manifest` does, afterwards, against the same local\n   202\t  store.\n   203\t\n   204\t### 4.3 The staged load\n   205\t\n   206\t`dao.space.dht/load` generalizes `load-index`. `dao.space.dht` learns nothing\n   207\tabout `yin.*`: the walk is an argument.\n   208\t\n   209\t```clojure\n   210\t(dao.space.dht/load node address {:kind k :walk f})   ; -> node\n   211\t(dao.space.dht/load-status node address)              ; -> status or nil\n   212\t(dao.space.dht/forget node address)                   ; -> node\n   213\t```\n   214\t\n   215\t`f` is `(fn [handle] outcome)`, called with the node's local store, and\n   216\tanswers one of three shapes:\n   217\t\n   218\t```clojure\n   219\t{:dao.space.dht/walk :missing  :address a}\n   220\t{:dao.space.dht/walk :complete :value v}\n   221\t{:dao.space.dht/walk :invalid  :address a-or-nil :defect {:code code ...}}\n   222\t```\n   223\t\n   224\t- A defect is a map with a keyword `:code` from the walk's own closed set.\n   225\t  The closure walk's set is section 4.2's; the index walk's is\n   226\t  `:index-invalid`. A defect may carry `:detail` (data) and `:text` (a\n   227\t  diagnostic string); no consumer compares either.\n   228\t- A function that throws is `:invalid` with the one defect\n   229\t  `{:code :dao.space.dht/walk-threw :text <the exception's message>}`. The\n   230\t  code is the contract; the text differs by host and is never matched.\n   231\t- An answer of any other shape is `:invalid` with\n   232\t  `{:code :dao.space.dht/walk-shape}`.\n   233\t- `load-index` is `load` with `:kind :dao.space.dht/index` and a walk that\n   234\t  adapts `index-datoms`; its behaviour, events and tests are unchanged.\n   235\t- On `:missing` the node requests the address with one `:jing/get` through\n   236\t  its `dao.jing.content.step` client and holds one outstanding fetch per\n   237\t  load. A fetched payload is verified against the requested address before\n   238\t  it enters `:local` (`dao.jing/accept-bytes!`, `dao.jing.dht.md` 4.5), and\n   239\t  the walk re-reads and re-checks it.\n   240\t- **Asking.** `dao.jing.content.step/request-get` answering `:busy` (the\n   241\t  client still owes an unsent request) is not a failure: the load stays\n   242\t  `:loading` with nothing fetching and asks again on the next step. Any\n   243\t  other outcome that submitted nothing fails the load `:unaskable` with\n   244\t  that outcome keyword verbatim.\n   245\t- A load is `:loaded` only when its walk answered `:complete`.\n   246\t- A load already `:loading`, `:loaded` or `:failed` is left as it is.\n   247\t  `forget` removes a terminal record; it is refused for a `:loading` one.\n   248\t\n   249\t**Load status:**\n   250\t\n   251\t```clojure\n   252\t{:status :loading :kind k :fetched n :fetching address-or-nil}\n   253\t{:status :loaded  :kind k :fetched n :value v}\n   254\t{:status :failed  :kind k :fetched n :reason reason}\n   255\t```\n   256\t\n   257\t**Failure reasons are data.** S5's strings are replaced, with no\n   258\tcompatibility path:\n   259\t\n   260\t| `:reason` | Meaning |\n   261\t|---|---|\n   262\t| `{:dao.space.dht/failure :miss :address a :cause c}` | No peer produced `a`. `c` is the `/miss` fact's reason for that address (`/exhausted`, `/deadline`, `/solo`, `/busy`), or `:dao.jing.dht/gap` when the fact was lost. |\n   263\t| `{:dao.space.dht/failure :invalid :address a-or-nil :defect {:code c ...}}` | The walk refused local content, threw, or answered another shape. |\n   264\t| `{:dao.space.dht/failure :unaskable :address a :outcome o}` | The request for `a` could not be submitted; `o` is the client's outcome keyword. |\n   265\t\n   266\tThese three are the whole set. Each has exactly one link response shape\n   267\t(section 9).\n   268\t\n   269\t**Events** (from `step`, each a plain map under `:dao.space.dht/event`):\n   270\t\n   271\t```clojure\n   272\t{:dao.space.dht/event :loaded      :manifest a :kind k :fetched n ...}\n   273\t{:dao.space.dht/event :load-failed :manifest a :kind k :reason reason}\n   274\t```\n   275\t\n   285\t\n   286\t### 5.1 Rows, every round (owner decision 1)\n   287\t\n   288\tThe code indexer (`yin.repl.index`) materializes every row of each expanded\n   289\tprogram packet it commits, through `yin.vm/materialize-tree!`, into the\n   290\tindex store, in the same round as that packet's transaction and before the\n   291\tround's publication is announced.\n   292\t\n   293\t- The rows of a round belong to that round's publication: they are put\n   294\t  through the node's store and are in the announced window.\n   295\t- A packet the indexer did not commit materializes nothing.\n   296\t- A row put that fails is recorded under the indexer's `:failure` with\n   297\t  stage `:materialize`; the round is not reported indexed.\n   298\t- Restored history is not re-materialized on rehydration. An index\n   299\t  published before this slice names rows its store does not hold, and\n   300\t  loading one such program by tree address is `:absent`.\n   301\t\n   302\t**The banner changes.** With `--dht-publish` the REPL states that the code\n   303\tindex *and the code itself* (every evaluated program's rows) are shared.\n   304\t\n   305\t**What the address fact promises.** An `:yin/address` fact names content the\n   306\tpublisher's own store holds from the round that committed it, and that a\n   307\tpublishing node serves to any peer that asks it. That much holds for every\n   308\tround, whatever the network did. Retrieval from peers other than the\n   309\tpublisher is promised only by a publication whose result is `:acknowledged`\n   310\t(section 5.5), and a result that is not names every address it does not\n   311\tcover.\n   312\t\n   313\tA round now puts one blob per row, far more than the DHT's pending-write\n   314\tbound. Section 5.5 fixes how those writes are paced, bounded and reported.\n   315\t\n   316\t### 5.2 A module closure\n   317\t\n   318\t`yin.vm.linker.publish/publish-module!` is the one manifest publisher. It is\n   319\tplain Clojure and host neutral, and takes any `dao.jing` handle.\n   320\t\n   321\t```clojure\n   322\t(publish-module! handle\n   323\t  {:name 'my.lib\n   324\t   :ast ast                       ; the canonical map AST\n   325\t   :exports #{'f 'g}\n   326\t   :requires {'other.lib manifest-address}\n   327\t   :primitives {'+ profile}})     ; yin.vm/profile-of's answer for the name\n   328\t;; ->\n   329\t{:address manifest-address\n   330\t :manifest {...}\n   331\t :identities {:yin.semantic/code v :yin.debruijn.code H :yin.debruijn.register R}\n   332\t :links {:yin.ast/code outcome :yin.semantic/code outcome\n   333\t         :yin.debruijn.code outcome :yin.debruijn.register outcome}}\n   334\t```\n   335\t\n   336\t- It materializes the tree rows, the semantic vector, the stack image, the\n   337\t  register image, one derivation record per lowered format under the pinned\n   338\t  profile (`ledger/lowering-profile`, `linker/stack-lowering-profile`,\n   339\t  `linker/register-lowering-profile`), and the schema-1 manifest with\n   340\t  `:yin.module/index` naming H and R.\n   341\t- **It always mints all four formats.** A manifest from this publisher has a\n   342\t  full closure.\n   343\t- **It links what it published.** After minting, it walks the closure\n   344\t  (must be `:complete`) and links each format locally under `:verifying`\n   345\t  with discharge deferred. `:links` carries each outcome: `{:status :ok}` or\n   346\t  the linker's refusal. A publisher learns before any peer does which\n   347\t  backends can link the module.\n   348\t- It refuses, writing nothing, when `:exports` names a symbol the tree does\n   349\t  not define at module level (`:yin.link.publish/undefined-export`).\n   350\t- Publishing twice writes nothing new: every blob is content-addressed.\n   351\t- The manifest's `:yin.module/primitives` holds each declared name's\n   352\t  `:yin.k/profile` address. `:yin.module/footprint` is computed, never\n   353\t  supplied.\n   354\t\n   355\t**The footprint.** `(yin.vm.linker.publish/footprint handle spec)` is a pure\n   356\tfunction of three inputs and answers the manifest's\n   357\t`:yin.module/footprint`:\n   358\t\n   359\t| Input | What is read |\n   360\t|---|---|\n   361\t| The module tree | `(yin.vm/ast-requirements db)` over the `$ast` relation of the tree's rows, `(vals (:rows (yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)))`: its `:store-keys`, `:effects`, `:ffi-ops` and `:parked-ids`. |\n   362\t| `:primitives` | Each declared profile's `:yin.k/effects`. |\n   363\t| `:requires` | Each required manifest, read from `handle`: its own `:yin.module/footprint` `:effects`. |\n   364\t\n   365\t```clojure\n   366\t{:store-keys <the tree's :store-keys>\n   367\t :effects    <the tree's :effects\n   368\t              ∪ every declared primitive's :yin.k/effects\n   369\t              ∪ every required manifest's footprint :effects>}\n   370\t```\n   371\t\n   372\t- `:store-keys` is the module's own: every constant store key its tree\n   373\t  reads, writes or defines, exactly the set `ast-requirements` answers. A\n   374\t  dependency's keys are not included; each module has its own store\n   375\t  (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3).\n   376\t- `:effects` is closed over dependencies by induction: a required\n   377\t  manifest's footprint already holds its own dependencies' effects.\n   378\t- A required manifest absent from `handle` refuses\n   379\t  `:yin.link.publish/missing-requirement` naming it. A declared primitive\n   380\t  with no profile refuses `:yin.link.publish/unprofiled-primitive`.\n   381\t- A tree whose requirements hold any `:ffi-ops` or `:parked-ids` refuses\n   382\t  `:yin.link.publish/ffi-op` or `:yin.link.publish/parked-id`. A schema-1\n   383\t  manifest has no key that declares either, so a receiver could not learn\n   384\t  of them.\n   385\t- Every function this reads exists on master. The footprint is not gated on\n   386\t  any unfinished work.\n   387\t\n   388\t### 5.3 Deriving a module from indexed code (R9)\n   389\t\n   390\t`yin.vm.linker.publish/module-from-index` builds `publish-module!`'s\n   437\t### 5.5 Replication: the backlog and the publication result\n   438\t\n   439\tThe node's store handle no longer asks the DHT to replicate at the moment of\n   440\ta put. A put inserts into `:local`, records the address on the node's ledger,\n   441\tand returns the local verdict. Oversize is still refused before the local\n   442\tinsert. `step` owns everything after that. No round waits, as before.\n   443\t\n   444\t**The backlog.** A node holds one FIFO backlog of addresses awaiting a\n   445\treplicate request.\n   446\t\n   447\t- **Admission** happens in `step`, when the ledger is read: each put\n   448\t  address is appended to the tail in ledger order. An address already in\n   449\t  the backlog or already outstanding is not added again; the publications\n   450\t  that wait on it share its one outcome.\n   451\t- **Capacity** is `:max-backlog` addresses (default 4096). An address that\n   452\t  arrives when the backlog is full is **not admitted**: it is settled at\n   453\t  once, for its publication, as refused with reason\n   454\t  `:dao.space.dht/backlog-full`. Its local copy stays. Nothing is evicted to\n   455\t  make room.\n   456\t- **Release.** Each step appends replicate requests from the head while the\n   457\t  number outstanding is below the DHT's `max-pending-writes`. The node never\n   458\t  has more outstanding than that bound, so its own writes never draw\n   459\t  `/busy`.\n   460\t- **Outcome.** `/sent` settles an address as sent. `/unacknowledged` with\n   461\t  `/too-few-peers` or `/busy` returns the address to the **tail** and\n   462\t  consumes one of its `:replicate-tries` (default 3); the last try settles\n   463\t  it refused with that reason. `/solo`, `/unpublished`, `/oversize` and\n   464\t  `/absent` settle it refused at once.\n   465\t- **Fairness.** Order is admission order, and a retry goes to the tail, so\n   466\t  an address that keeps failing never holds the head and an older\n   467\t  publication's first tries precede a newer one's. No publication is\n   468\t  prioritized.\n   469\t- **A solo or non-publishing node holds no backlog.** Every put settles at\n   470\t  admission as `/solo` or `/unpublished`.\n   471\t- **Cancellation.** `(dao.space.dht/cancel! node manifest-address)` removes\n   472\t  that publication's addresses that no other publication waits on from the\n   473\t  backlog, settles them refused with `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`, and reports\n   474\t  the publication at once; a late fact for a cancelled address is ignored.\n   475\t  `close!` discards the backlog and reports nothing; local copies are\n   476\t  durable. Nothing cancels a publication implicitly: a newer round does not\n   477\t  supersede an older one's writes.\n   478\t- **A lost ledger entry** (more puts in one window than the ledger ring\n   479\t  holds, or a facts gap) reports `:publication-unknown`, as today.\n   480\t\n   481\t**The result.** A publication is reported exactly once, when every one of\n   482\tits blobs is settled.\n   483\t\n   484\t```clojure\n   485\t{:dao.space.dht/event :published\n   486\t :manifest m\n   487\t :result :acknowledged | :partial | :unacknowledged\n   488\t :blobs n            ; distinct addresses in the publication\n   489\t :sent s             ; settled /sent\n   490\t :peers p            ; fewest peers any sent blob reached; absent when s = 0\n   491\t :failed [{:address a :reason r :peers k} ...]}   ; every blob not sent\n   492\t```\n   493\t\n   494\t| `:result` | Condition | What it promises |\n   495\t|---|---|---|\n   496\t| `:acknowledged` | Every blob sent. | Every address of the publication was handed to at least `ack-peers` peers. |\n   497\t| `:partial` | The manifest blob sent; at least one other blob not. | Peers can find the publication. The addresses under `:failed` are held and served by the publisher only. |\n   498\t| `:unacknowledged` | The manifest blob not sent. | Nothing beyond the local copy. `:failed` still lists every blob not sent. |\n   499\t\n   500\t- `:acknowledged?` is removed, with no compatibility path.\n   501\t- `:failed` is complete, in address order. The node keeps the last\n   502\t  `:max-retained-failures` (default 16) non-acknowledged results for\n   503\t  `retry!`; an older one is dropped whole and cannot be retried by\n   504\t  manifest.\n   505\t- **The announced index is unaffected.** HEAD moves when the round's blobs\n   506\t  are local and the manifest reads back, before any network result, exactly\n   507\t  as in S5. A `:partial` or `:unacknowledged` result retracts nothing.\n   508\t- **Effect on a reader.** A reader that loads an address under some\n   509\t  publication's `:failed` reaches it only if its lookup asks the publisher.\n   510\t  Otherwise its load fails `:miss` with the DHT's cause, as data. It never\n   511\t  loads wrong content and never hangs.\n   512\t- **Repair is explicit.**\n   513\t  `(dao.space.dht/retry! node manifest-address)` re-admits exactly the\n   514\t  retained `:failed` addresses of that manifest, with fresh tries, as a new\n   515\t  publication of the same manifest, reported again by the same rules. It is\n   516\t  refused for a manifest with no retained result. Nothing retries\n   517\t  automatically.\n   518\t- The REPL prints the result and, when it is not `:acknowledged`, the count\n   519\t  of failed blobs, the first reason, and the `retry` call. Its\n   520\t  `dao.space.dht` host module exposes `retry` and `cancel` over the two\n   521\t  plain functions.\n   522\t- **A module published at the prompt** shares its round's publication. Its\n   523\t  name assertion is in the index whether or not its closure replicated; a\n   524\t  reader of a `:partial` publication may resolve the name and fail the load\n   525\t  with a `:miss`. The publisher is told which blobs to retry.\n   526\t\n   527\t## 6. Names: envelopes, proofs, keys\n   528\t\n   529\t### 6.1 The envelope\n   530\t\n   744\t### 7.4 Transitive requires\n   745\t\n   746\tA module's `:yin.module/requires` pins each dependency's manifest address,\n   747\tand discharge accepts only a linked module of that address. The closure\n   748\twalker loads the pinned closure. The install child still requires the\n   749\tdependency **by name**. So each dependency name must resolve, in the\n   750\treader's own name environment, to the address the publisher pinned.\n   751\t\n   752\t**A dependency name is resolved exactly as any name is: by the fold of 7.3\n   753\tover the reader's snapshot set, under the reader's declared principals.**\n   754\t\n   755\t- No index is privileged. The dependency's assertion may be by any declared\n   756\t  principal, in any snapshot of the set. The dependent module's index need\n   757\t  not hold it, and a publisher asserts only the names it publishes.\n   758\t- Nothing is copied. A publisher does not relay, re-sign or re-assert\n   759\t  another principal's envelope, and a pinned address in a manifest is never\n   760\t  a name claim: it says what the publisher linked against, not who may bind\n   761\t  the name.\n   762\t- A dependency published by another principal therefore reaches a reader in\n   763\t  one way: the reader is handed that principal's index manifest address and\n   764\t  loads it (`load-index`), and declares that principal\n   765\t  (`--dht-principal`). Both are the reader's composition, as for any name.\n   766\t- A direct entry (`:name-env`, `--link-name`) binds a dependency name like\n   767\t  any other.\n   768\t\n   769\t**The binding check.** `(yin.vm.linker.dht/dependency-bindings node authority\n   770\tmanifest-address)` reads the loaded closure's `:requires` (section 4.2) and\n   771\tanswers one entry per requirement, in walk order:\n   772\t\n   773\t```clojure\n   774\t{:module requiring-manifest :name 'other.lib :pinned a\n   775\t :binding :ok}\n   776\t{:module requiring-manifest :name 'other.lib :pinned a\n   777\t :binding :absent    :diagnostics [...]}\n   778\t{:module requiring-manifest :name 'other.lib :pinned a\n   779\t :binding :ambiguous :addresses [...] :asserters [...]}\n   780\t{:module requiring-manifest :name 'other.lib :pinned a\n   781\t :binding :mismatch  :resolved b :asserters [...]}\n   782\t```\n   783\t\n   784\t- `:ok` is: the name resolves to exactly the pinned address. Several\n   785\t  declared principals asserting that one address is `:ok` (R8).\n   786\t- `:absent` is: no accepted assertion. Its diagnostics are the fold's for\n   787\t  that name, so an assertion by an undeclared principal, or one in an\n   788\t  index the reader has not loaded, is told apart from no assertion at all\n   789\t  only by what the loaded snapshots hold.\n   790\t- `:ambiguous` is: more than one distinct address among accepted\n   791\t  assertions, whether or not the pinned one is among them.\n   792\t- `:mismatch` is: one address, not the pinned one. A dependency its\n   793\t  publisher has since republished is this case.\n   794\t- The DHT link source runs the check after the load and before the link\n   795\t  (section 4.1). The first entry that is not `:ok` refuses the require with\n   796\t  `:yin.link.dht/dependency-binding` carrying that entry. No install child\n   797\t  starts.\n   798\t- The check is the early, named form of what discharge would refuse later\n   799\t  as `:unresolved-free`; it adds no rule the linker lacks.\n   800\t\n   801\t## 8. Evaluation\n   802\t\n   803\t### 8.1 Entry\n   804\t\n   805\tLoaded code enters a session by the flow of `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.2 and 7.3,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
