{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '975,1030p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v975; sed -n '1103,1135p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v1103; sed -n '1180,1202p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v1180; git diff -- docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md\"","aggregated_output":"   975\t**L1 — the staged load, the backlog, the publication result.** Files:\n   976\t`src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc`,\n   977\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc` (reason and result rendering only),\n   978\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc` (status shape, `retry`, `cancel`),\n   979\t`test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc`, `test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc`,\n   980\t`test/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj` (the result's printed line),\n   981\t`docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md` (section 10's event list). Acceptance:\n   982\t- `load-index` is `load`; every existing index-load test passes with\n   983\t  reasons asserted as data.\n   984\t- A walk answering `:missing` fetches; `:complete` loads; `:invalid` fails\n   985\t  without any fetch. A throwing walk fails with code\n   986\t  `:dao.space.dht/walk-threw` and a walk answering another shape with\n   987\t  `:dao.space.dht/walk-shape`; both codes are asserted equal on the JVM,\n   988\t  Node and Dart, and no test compares `:text`.\n   989\t- `request-get` answering `:busy` leaves the load `:loading` and it\n   990\t  completes on a later step. A client that cannot submit fails the load\n   991\t  `:unaskable` with the outcome keyword, the same value on all three hosts.\n   992\t- **Pacing.** One round of 500 puts against acknowledging peers is\n   993\t  `:acknowledged`; the number of outstanding replicate requests never\n   994\t  exceeds `max-pending-writes` at any step, and no `/busy` fact is\n   995\t  produced.\n   996\t- **Sustained rounds.** Eight consecutive rounds of 500 puts (within\n   997\t  `:max-backlog` in total), announced faster than they drain, are each\n   998\t  reported once, in announcement order, all `:acknowledged`; the backlog\n   999\t  returns to empty; `busy?` then answers false. A ninth and tenth round\n  1000\t  announced before any drain exceed the bound: their excess blobs are\n  1001\t  `:dao.space.dht/backlog-full` and those rounds are not `:acknowledged`.\n  1002\t- **A peer that never accepts.** With every peer refusing or never\n  1003\t  answering stores, sustained rounds end, by appended ticks alone, with\n  1004\t  every publication reported `:unacknowledged`, each blob under `:failed`\n  1005\t  with `/too-few-peers` after exactly `:replicate-tries` tries; rounds that\n  1006\t  arrive at a full backlog report their excess blobs\n  1007\t  `:dao.space.dht/backlog-full` in the step that reads them; the backlog\n  1008\t  never exceeds its bound; no put ever waits; `busy?` ends false.\n  1009\t- **Fairness.** With one address that always fails and others that\n  1010\t  succeed, every other address of every publication is sent before that\n  1011\t  address's last try.\n  1012\t- **The result.** A mesh that refuses the store of one chosen non-manifest\n  1013\t  blob reports `:partial` with exactly that address under `:failed`; one\n  1014\t  that refuses the manifest blob reports `:unacknowledged`; `:sent` plus\n  1015\t  the count of `:failed` equals `:blobs` in every result. HEAD has moved in\n  1016\t  each case.\n  1017\t- `retry!` after the mesh heals re-reports that manifest `:acknowledged`;\n  1018\t  it is refused for a manifest with no retained result, and for one beyond\n  1019\t  `:max-retained-failures`.\n  1020\t- `cancel!` reports the publication at once with its unsent blobs\n  1021\t  `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`, leaves an address another publication waits\n  1022\t  on in the backlog, and ignores a late fact. `close!` reports nothing and\n  1023\t  leaves every blob local.\n  1024\t- A solo node and a non-publishing node hold no backlog and report\n  1025\t  `/solo` and `/unpublished` in the step that reads the puts.\n  1026\t- Each of `/exhausted`, `/deadline`, `/solo` and `/busy` reaches the\n  1027\t  `:failed` reason as `:cause`.\n  1028\t- A peer serving bytes that do not hash to the address never produces\n  1029\t  `:loaded`; the blob is not in `:local`.\n  1030\t- Exactly one terminal event per load. `forget` clears a terminal record\n  1103\t**L4 — rows every round, names in the index, publish at the prompt.**\n  1104\tFiles: `src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc`,\n  1105\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc` (the `yin.link` host\n  1106\tmodule), `src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`,\n  1107\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc` (flags, banner, keygen),\n  1108\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc` (`module-from-index`),\n  1109\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc` (`snapshots`, `names`), the tests beside\n  1110\teach, and the design docs this one cross-references. Acceptance:\n  1111\t- After a round, every row of the evaluated program is in the index store,\n  1112\t  and a second node loads that program's tree by its `:yin.repl/root`\n  1113\t  address.\n  1114\t- A program of more rows than the DHT's pending-write bound is published\n  1115\t  `:acknowledged`, never `/busy`, and so are eight such rounds in a row.\n  1116\t- **A failed row replication.** With one row's store refused by every\n  1117\t  peer, the round is indexed, HEAD moves, and the publication is `:partial`\n  1118\t  naming that row. A second node loads the index, and its load of that\n  1119\t  program's tree fails `:miss` on exactly that address when its lookup does\n  1120\t  not reach the publisher, and succeeds when it does. After `retry` against\n  1121\t  healed peers the publication is `:acknowledged` and the load succeeds.\n  1122\t  The REPL's line names the result, the failed count and the retry call.\n  1123\t- A module published at the prompt in a `:partial` round: a reader\n  1124\t  resolves its name, and its require is refused `:absent` with the miss\n  1125\t  cause, as data, until the publisher retries.\n  1126\t- A two-principal dependency at the prompt: `app` requiring `base` from\n  1127\t  another publisher evaluates when the reader has loaded both indexes and\n  1128\t  declared both principals, and raises `:yin.link.dht/dependency-binding`\n  1129\t  with `:absent`, `:mismatch` and `:ambiguous` in the three cases of L2.\n  1130\t- `(yin.link/publish 'my.lib '[f])` over two defining programs, one free\n  1131\t  primitive and one linker-required module produces one tree in `t` order\n  1132\t  and a manifest declaring both; an undefined export, an undeclared free\n  1133\t  name, a host-module name and a missing key each refuse and write nothing.\n  1134\t- Republishing a name with a new manifest writes the retraction and the\n  1135\t  assertion; a reader of the new snapshot resolves the new address; a\n  1180\t- **Resolving a transitive require by its pinned address**, and delivery of\n  1181\t  a dependency closure as one response (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.4).\n  1182\t- **Relaying a foreign assertion.** A signed envelope is carrier\n  1183\t  independent, so a publisher could copy a dependency's envelope and proof\n  1184\t  into its own index and spare the reader one index load. The reader would\n  1185\t  still have to declare the foreign principal. Not built; section 7.4\n  1186\t  forbids it to this epic's publisher.\n  1187\t- **Automatic repair of a non-acknowledged publication.** Repair is the\n  1188\t  explicit `retry!` of section 5.5.\n  1189\t- **FFI operations and parked ids in a published module.** Schema 1 cannot\n  1190\t  declare them; the publisher refuses.\n  1191\t- **Step 5a for module-level reads in the tree formats.** Which modules\n  1192\t  link on the walker and semantic VMs is the linker's rule, not this\n  1193\t  epic's.\n  1194\t- **Host modules as dependencies of a published module.**\n  1195\t- **Availability repair.** Pinning, re-replication of content whose holders\n  1196\t  left, and garbage collection are `dao.jing.dht.md`'s deferrals.\n  1197\t- **A node id derived from the publisher's key** (`dao.jing.dht.md` 6).\n  1198\t- **ShiBi.** Declarations and write capabilities as tuples supplied to the\n  1199\t  composition.\n  1200\t- **Signed names in a browser build.**\n  1201\t\n  1202\t## Lineage\ndiff --git a/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md b/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md\nindex 0123a556..e425be59 100644\n--- a/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md\n+++ b/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md\n@@ -667,6 +667,15 @@ Out of this epic: pinning, garbage collection, availability repair and\n reconciliation sweeps, NAT meeting, a browser transport, latest-root\n discovery, authenticated node ids.\n \n+**Loading code for evaluation** is the next epic, specified in\n+[`yin.vm.linker.dht.md`](./yin.vm.linker.dht.md). It generalizes\n+`load-index` into `dao.space.dht/load` (a staged load over any walk, with\n+failure reasons as data), adds a bounded replicate backlog so a round's\n+many row writes never report `/busy`, replaces the `:published` event's\n+`:acknowledged?` with a result of `:acknowledged`, `:partial` or\n+`:unacknowledged` that lists every blob not sent, and makes the node's\n+store the linker's content source. It changes nothing in sections 2 to 9.\n+\n ## Relationship to the intake-pool observer\n \n The DHT is a content-store backend, not a stream transport. The intake-pool\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"## Verdict: SIGN-OFF WITHHELD\n\nR2 closes the dependency-binding, `:unaskable`, closed defect-code, and footprint findings. Moving backlog and result work to L1 is sound: L4 can then test row publication against an established API. Refusing FFI and parked-id modules is consistent with the stated schema-1 limit, and L0 tests those refusals. The owner’s explicit decisions and architectural invariants remain intact.\n\nThe bounded backlog closes the capacity finding, but its cancellation and retry rules still conflict with the publication result contract.\n\n| Severity | File:line | Issue | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| High | [yin.vm.linker.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-linker-dht/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md:471) | `cancel!` settles backlog entries and reports the publication “at once,” but it does not define how writes already outstanding are counted. Those writes can later produce `/sent`; the result’s `:sent`, `:failed`, and `:blobs` may then disagree. L1 explicitly expects a late fact, so this is observable. | Specify whether cancellation marks outstanding writes as cancelled for this publication, waits for their facts, or reports a distinct cancellation result. Define shared-address accounting and assert `:sent + count(:failed) = :blobs` after cancellation. |\n| High | [yin.vm.linker.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-linker-dht/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md:512) | `retry!` re-admits **only failed addresses** as a new publication of the same manifest. When the manifest succeeded in the original partial result, it is absent from the retry. The stated result rules classify that retry as `:unacknowledged`, while L1 and L4 require `:acknowledged` after repair. | Define retry as an update to the original publication’s complete blob ledger, or define a separate repair result. State how the original sent blobs contribute to the final acknowledgment and test both partial and unacknowledged originals. |\n\n**Owner questions**\n\n1. Should a partial or unacknowledged publication require an explicit `retry!`, as r2 specifies, or should the node retry automatically while it remains open? Both fit the current owner decisions; this availability policy needs the owner’s call."}}
